<title>BRAZIL: Brazil key soybean states to see less rain - Somar.</title>
<headline>Brazil key soybean states to see less rain - Somar.</headline>
<dateline>RIO DE JANEIRO 1996-11-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>Brazil's key southern soybean states of Parana and Rio Grande do Sul should see less rain as the week goes on, leading to more favorable planting conditions, said independent meteorologists Somar.</p>
<p>However, further north the weather will remain cloudy and rainy, with thunderstorms forecast for Mato Grosso, it added.</p>
<p>The past week has seen rain throughout the soybean producing region, with the highest rainfall around Londrina and Cascavel in Parana state. Temperatures will remain on the high side.</p>
<p>Rainfall in the past week and the historical monthly</p>
<p>average in key states in millimeters was as follows.</p>
<p> STATE		   TOTAL RAIN   TOTAL RAIN   TOTAL   AVERAGE</p>
<p>		     Nov 12-18     Nov 5-11  Nov 1-18  November</p>
<p> Londrina (PR)	   129.2	    3.0     197.4     120</p>
<p> Cascaval (PR)	   122.9	    5.0     160.4     125</p>
<p> Passo Fundo (RS)	  6.5	   11.0	72.1     110</p>
<p> Sao Luiz Gonzaga (RS)  24.2	   37.0	84.7     110</p>
<p> Santa Maria (RS)	 14.1	   15.0	44.2     111</p>
<p> Mato Grosso		64.0	   10.0	99.0     164</p>
<p>Brazilian soybean plantings were 43 percent completed by</p>
<p>November 14 against 34 percent by November 16, 1995 and an</p>
<p>average of the past five years of 36 percent, consultants</p>
<p>Safras e Mercado said today.</p>
<p>-- Caroline Bulloch, Rio de Janeiro newsroom, 5521 5074151</p>
</text>
